<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brian Boru</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 941–<a href="/years/1014">1014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Brian Bóruma, Brian Bóruma mac Cennétig</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brian Boru was King of Munster from the Dál Cais dynasty and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a> from 1002. He broke the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> towns and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a>, then forced the other provincial kings to accept his overlordship. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf">Battle of Clontarf</a> in 1014, where his army defeated Leinster and its Norse allies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brian-boru.jpg" alt="Brian Boru" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brian_B%C3%B3ruma_mac_Cenn%C3%A9tig_(1723)_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Brian Boru was one of the twelve sons of Cennétig mac Lorcáin, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-cais">Dál gCais</a> and of Tuadmumu (Thomond), in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-clare">County Clare</a>. He was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kincora">Kincora</a>, his father's residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/killaloe">Killaloe</a>, and as the youngest of twelve he was highly unlikely to inherit. He was sent to study <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Irish history at the monastery on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inisfallen">Innisfallen</a>, under the monk Maelsuthain Ua Cearbhaill, and was brought home at ten when his father was killed in battle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>. The name Bóruma was given to him after his death; it may refer to a fort north of Killaloe, or to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> tribute of a powerful overlord.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 964 his older brother Mathgamain captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-of-cashel">Rock of Cashel</a> and claimed the kingship of Munster, which the weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a> had held. The brothers routed the Norse king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivar-of-limerick">Ivar of Limerick</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sulcoit">Battle of Sulcoit</a> in 968. After Mathgamain's death Brian succeeded him as king of Thomond, killed Ivar in 977, and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> who had taken refuge on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scattery-island">Scattery Island</a>, slaughtering them and desecrating the church. In 978 he defeated the Eóganacht claimant Máel Muad at the Battle of Belach Lechta, and the kingship of Munster passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-cais">Dál gCais</a>.</p><p>Brian then turned to Leinster and Connacht and came into conflict with the High King, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mael-sechnaill-mac-domnaill">Máel Sechnaill mac Domnaill</a>. In the year 1000 he led a combined Munster, Leinster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> army into Meath. After the defeat at Glenmama in 999, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitric-silkbeard">Sigtrygg Silkbeard</a> of Dublin had submitted to him. Máel Sechnaill acknowledged Brian as High King at Athlone in 1002.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In his sixties Brian spent about a decade accepting the submission of the lesser kingdoms, including two full circuits of the island in 1005 and 1006. Ulster, the one province that did not recognise him, took ten years of campaigning to subdue. In 1012 Máel Mórda mac Murchada of Leinster rebelled, and in 1013 Flaithbertach Ua Néill raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>.</p><p>In 1014 Brian's army met the forces of Leinster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf">Battle of Clontarf</a>. Accounts of his death differ: some say he died in single combat, others that he was too old to fight. According to the Cogadh Gaedhel re Gallaibh he was killed by fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> mercenaries while kneeling on a cushion away from the fighting. Historians debate whether Clontarf ended a war between the Irish and the Vikings or was an Irish civil war with Vikings on both sides. His descendants were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrien-dynasty">Uí Briain</a>, or O'Briens.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brian Boru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Norse raiders built a fortified base at Dublin in 841, which grew into the chief Norse town and slave market of Ireland; <b>Brian Boru</b>’s victory at Clontarf in 1014 was later remembered as the end of Viking power there.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish high king <b>Brian Boru</b> defeated the forces of Leinster and their Norse allies from Dublin and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-23">23 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Probably near Lyons Hill in County Kildare, the forces of Munster and Meath under <b>Brian Boru</b> and the High King Máel Sechnaill II crushed the armies of Leinster and Dublin led by King Máel Mórda, ending the brief Leinster revolt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-30">30 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A northern branch, the Déis Tuaiscirt, later grew into the Dál Cais, the dynasty of <b>Brian Bóruma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/deisi-muman">Deisi Muman</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Brian Boru</b> of Munster broke the Ui Neill monopoly around 1002, and later kings ruled 'with opposition'.</blockquote><a href="/state/high-kingship-of-ireland">High Kingship of Ireland</a></li><li><blockquote>Mathgamain and <b>Brian Boru</b> of the Dal Cais defeated the Norse at Sulcoit in 967 and sacked the town.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-limerick">Kingdom of Limerick</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Killaloe, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Clontarf, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.8,-8.45&amp;year=988" aria-label="Killaloe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4361 344 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Brian Boru"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4811 614h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4811 614h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.8,-8.45&amp;year=988">What was here in 988 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
